          "ruleId": "CWE-770",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unbounded parser buffer growth in JsonTokenizer.fillBuf(): a single oversized JSON token drives repeated capacity doubling with no ceiling, giving attacker-controlled memory exhaustion. Attacker input enters at `reader.read(buf, storeEnd, buf.length - storeEnd)` (JsonTokenizer.java:495). readString() accumulates the token body into `buf[storeEnd++]` (lines 152-154) without ever flushing, so the stored-token length grows with the attacker's token. Once `storeLen == buf.length` (line 475), fillBuf() executes `char[] doubleBuf = Arrays.copyOf(buf, 2 * buf.length)` (line 477) -- there is no maximum-capacity check anywhere on this path. A client POSTing one JSON document containing a single huge string value (e.g. {\"a\":\"AAAA...\"}) therefore forces the char[] to double repeatedly until OutOfMemoryError. Confirmed there is no bounding config: org.eclipse.parsson.api.JsonConfig declares only MAX_BIGINTEGER_SCALE, MAX_BIGDECIMAL_LEN, MAX_DEPTH and REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS -- no string-length, token-length or document-size limit exists to cap this allocation.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: fillBuf() grows the token buffer by unconditional doubling with no maximum-capacity check and no overflow guard (JsonTokenizer.java:475-479, `Arrays.copyOf(buf, 2 * buf.length)`), fed directly by attacker-controlled input at `reader.read(buf, storeEnd, buf.length-storeEnd)` (495). readString() accumulates the whole token body into buf (`buf[storeEnd]=(char)ch; storeEnd++`, 152-154) with no flush, and unescape() does the same (174-203), so the stored-token length \u2014 and hence the allocation \u2014 is bounded only by the attacker's token length. I confirmed myself that no bounding knob exists anywhere: api/JsonConfig.java:19-52 declares only MAX_BIGINTEGER_SCALE, MAX_BIGDECIMAL_LEN, MAX_DEPTH, REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS, and a repo-wide grep for maxStringLength/MAX_STRING/maxTokenLength/maxDocument returns no matches; the only length check in the file (521, bigDecimalLengthLimit) gates BigDecimal *conversion*, not buffering. The path is reachable from the public API: JsonParserImpl.java:67,74,80 construct the tokenizer from a caller-supplied Reader/InputStream, nextToken() calls readString() at 355, readString() calls read() at 149, read() calls fillBuf() at 456; graph confirms reachable_from_entry=true for all of lines 152,154,456,475,477,495. So the structural claim (CWE-770/789, unbounded allocation driven by untrusted input) is real and reachable. I downgrade the analyst's impact framing and severity, however: reset() runs per token (nextToken():332), so buf only reaches the largest single token rather than accumulating, and after each doubling only buf.length-storeEnd further chars can be read, so growth is linear in attacker bytes \u2014 peak heap is roughly 3 bytes per ASCII byte sent, with no amplification, and BufferPoolImpl holds recycled oversized buffers only behind a WeakReference (BufferPoolImpl.java:32,70) so there is no persistent retention. A real but LOW-severity missing-resource-limit hardening gap (plus a secondary int-overflow at 477: 2*buf.length goes negative at buf.length>=2^30, throwing NegativeArraySizeException instead of a JsonException). Exploit-reference check was not possible \u2014 exploit_search reported the corpus unavailable/not indexed; version pinned at 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (pom.xml:33), which already contains the CVE-2023-4043-class number limits and MAX_DEPTH, so only the string/token dimension remains uncapped."
          },
