        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-770",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JsonTokenizer has NO maximum token/string length control: fillBuf() doubles the internal char[] without any upper bound (`char[] doubleBuf = Arrays.copyOf(buf, 2 * buf.length)`), so a single oversized JSON string token drives unbounded heap allocation. The only length control in the library, bigDecimalLengthLimit, is applied in getBigDecimal() at line 521 \u2014 i.e. AFTER the buffer has already been grown to hold the whole token \u2014 and it covers numbers only, never strings. JsonContext exposes no max-string-length / max-token-length property at all (its entire control surface is depthLimit, bigIntegerScaleLimit, bigDecimalLengthLimit, prettyPrinting, rejectDuplicateKeys, bufferPool). Aggravating factor: the grown buffer is handed back to the shared pool via `jsonContext.bufferPool().recycle(buf)` at line 583, so one oversized document permanently inflates the process-wide BufferPool. This sits on the core untrusted-input path (JsonReaderImpl/JsonParserImpl -> JsonTokenizer), including the JAX-RS JsonValueBodyReader.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. JsonTokenizer.fillBuf() grows its char[] via `Arrays.copyOf(buf, 2 * buf.length)` (line 477) guarded only by `storeLen == buf.length` (line 475), with no comparison against any configured maximum \u2014 and grep across impl/src/main/java shows this is the ONLY array-growth site in the tokenizer. readString() (128-168) accumulates an entire string token between storeBegin/storeEnd, so one oversized string token forces repeated doubling until the whole token is resident, bounded only by the heap. No string/token-length control exists to stop it: JsonContext's full accessor set (112-134) is bigIntegerScaleLimit, bigDecimalLengthLimit, depthLimit, prettyPrinting, rejectDuplicateKeys, bufferPool, and api/JsonConfig.java (19-52) publishes only MAX_BIGINTEGER_SCALE, MAX_BIGDECIMAL_LEN, MAX_DEPTH, REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS \u2014 a defending application has no knob. The single length limit is both post-allocation and number-only: getBigDecimal() (518-527) derives sourceLen from storeEnd-storeBegin, i.e. after the token is fully buffered, so even the number path permits the unbounded char[] and only blocks BigDecimal construction. Reachability holds: the line-477 call is reachable_from_entry=true (hop 9) and readString/read/fillBuf are all reachable=true, with a concrete untrusted remote path through the JAX-RS JsonValueBodyReader. TWO ANALYST CLAIMS CORRECTED: (1) the \"permanently inflates the process-wide BufferPool\" aggravator is wrong \u2014 BufferPoolImpl.java:32 holds the queue as a WeakReference with no strong referent, so the pool and any oversized recycled buffer are GC-collectible exactly under the heap pressure this attack induces; retention is a transient window, not permanent (also, line 478 recycles the OLD smaller buffer; line 583 in close() returns the grown one). (2) Amplification is linear (~3-6x transmitted bytes: up to 2N-char array from doubling overshoot plus the getValue() String copy), not an explosive small-payload bomb, and container body-size limits commonly gate it \u2014 hence MEDIUM, not HIGH. Version pinned at 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (pom.xml:33); the CVE-2023-4043 number/depth mitigations are already present, so this is a distinct unaddressed gap. Exploit catalog returned \"not indexed\", so no exploit-reference signal was available in either direction and none is claimed."
          },
