        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-789",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JsonPatchImpl.DiffGenerator.diffArray() allocates a quadratic LCS matrix `int[][] c = new int[m+1][n+1]` where m and n are the sizes of the two attacker-supplied JSON arrays, with NO size cap or product check anywhere. There is no configurable limit for this in JsonConfig/JsonContext at all (unlike depth and number length), so Json.createDiff(source, target) on two moderately large arrays allocates m*n ints: two 50,000-element arrays request ~2.5e9 ints (~10 GB) and OOM the JVM from a request body of only a few hundred KB \u2014 a very high amplification factor.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently verified against source. JsonPatchImpl.DiffGenerator.diffArray() reads both attacker-controlled array lengths and immediately allocates a quadratic LCS matrix with no bound: `int m = source.size(); int n = target.size(); int[][] c = new int[m+1][n+1];` (lines 270-272). No guard exists between the size() reads and the alloc. I confirmed no limit is available anywhere: JsonConfig declares exactly four knobs (MAX_BIGINTEGER_SCALE:28, MAX_BIGDECIMAL_LEN:35, MAX_DEPTH:41, REJECT_DUPLICATE_KEYS:50) and JsonContext:54-60 holds only those three ints \u2014 none covers array size or diff cost; grepping `limit|size()` across the whole of JsonPatchImpl.java returns only lines 270-271, so the jsonContext field (:217) is never consulted for bounds. Reachability is established despite reachable_from_entry=false on line 272: the graph has JsonProviderImpl.createDiff as an :EntryPoint with exposure=\"reachable\"/reachable_from_entry=true, DiffGenerator.diff (line 223) at hop_distance=4, and a diff:<unresolvedSignature>(3) stub at hop_distance=6 showing the private 3-arg diff is reached \u2014 the false label on diffArray is overload-resolution loss at the diff->diffArray dispatch, which the source closes unambiguously (JsonProviderImpl.java:177 -> JsonPatchImpl.java:141 -> :225 -> :229 -> :237-238 -> :264). Amplification is as claimed: two 50,000-element arrays are a few hundred KB of body but request 50001*50001 ints (~10 GB) plus 50,001 array headers; even 20,000 per side (~200 KB body) demands ~1.6 GB, OOMing a normal heap. A secondary defect reinforces the DoS: emit() (:291-324) recurses to depth O(m+n), so inputs too small to OOM can still StackOverflowError. I rate MEDIUM not HIGH because exploitation requires the host application to call Json.createDiff on two untrusted structures, which is much rarer than the untrusted-parsing path. exploit_search was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so although the version pins to Parsson 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (pom.xml:33) I make no patched/unpatched claim from the exploit catalog; the verdict rests solely on source evidence I gathered."
          },
