        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-674",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JsonGeneratorImpl enforces no depth limit on output: its context stack is a plain unbounded `Deque stack = new ArrayDeque<>()` (line 86) pushed by writeStartObject/writeStartArray with no limit check, and write(JsonValue) is directly self-recursive over nested children (line 240 -> 249 `write(child)`; line 312 -> 331 `write(member.getKey(), member.getValue())`) with no depth parameter or counter. jsonContext.depthLimit() is never consulted in this file, so serializing a deeply nested JsonStructure recurses until StackOverflowError. This is reachable from the public writer API: JsonWriterImpl line 67 calls `this.generator.write(value)` in a loop over the structure.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The code defect the lead describes is exactly present and I re-derived it from source. JsonGeneratorImpl keeps an unbounded `Deque<Context> stack = new ArrayDeque<>()` (line 86) and pushes at lines 129/142/294/307 guarded only by scope-validity checks, never a depth check. write(JsonValue) (240-282) is self-recursive at 249 and mutually recursive into write(String,JsonValue) (312-354) at 257, which recurses at 323/331 -- no depth parameter or counter anywhere. A tree-wide case-insensitive grep for \"depth\" proves jsonContext.depthLimit() is consulted ONLY in JsonParserImpl (66/72/79) and never in JsonGeneratorImpl.java, so the write path has no depth control (CWE-674, uncontrolled recursion). Reachability is real: JsonWriterImpl:67 (`generator.write(value)`) and :89, plus JsonObjectImpl.toString() at JsonObjectBuilderImpl.java:329, and the graph marks the recursive calls at 249/257/323/331 reachable_from_entry=true (hop_distance 3-5). I am downgrading severity to LOW because the analyst omitted a real mitigating control: parsed input is depth-capped at DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH=1000 (JsonContext.java:44) enforced in JsonParserImpl.Stack.push (lines 394-397, \"Input is too deeply nested\"), and JsonReaderImpl.read/readObject/readArray (61-108) all go through parser.getObject()/getArray(), so attacker-supplied JSON yields at most ~999 nesting levels -- roughly 1000 small frames, which does not overflow a default JVM thread stack (JsonNestingTest confirms 500 doc-levels throws, 499 succeeds). Reaching StackOverflowError therefore requires a builder-constructed structure (JsonObjectBuilderImpl/MapUtil.handle, MapUtil.java:38-70, are mutually recursive with no depth cap) or an application that explicitly raises org.eclipse.parsson.maxDepth. So: a genuine, confirmed write-side hardening gap, not a directly weaponizable DoS under default configuration. No exploit-reference cross-check was possible -- exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"; version pinned at parsson 1.1.3-SNAPSHOT (pom.xml:33), a tree that already contains the parse-depth fix tied to CVE-2023-4043."
          },
          "locations": [
