        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-319",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Transport protection is applied on two of three outbound-API demo paths and silently omitted on the third, where it also leaks a credential. demos/rest/.../ParserResource.java line 53 builds `new URL(\"http://api.flickr.com/services/rest/?method=flickr.photos.getRecent&api_key=221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77&format=json&nojsoncallback=1&per_page=20\")` -- plaintext HTTP carrying a hardcoded Flickr api_key in the query string. The sibling demos in the same repo DO use TLS for the identical pattern: TwitterObjectSearch.java:69 `String searchUrl = \"https://api.twitter.com/1.1/search/tweets.json\"` and FacebookObjectSearch.java:67 `String searchUrl = \"https://graph.facebook.com/search?q=tamil&type=post&access_token=\"`. So the same codebase demonstrably knows to use https for credentialed API calls and does not on this one path, exposing the api_key to any network observer and allowing the JSON response to be tampered with in transit -- and that response is then parsed and reflected into an HTML page (line 74 `ps.println(\"<img src=\\\"http://farm\" + farm + \".staticflickr.com/\" + server + \"/\" + id + \"_\" + secret + \".jpg\\\">\")` where farm/server/id/secret come from parser.getString(), e.g. line 82 `secret = parser.getString()`), so an MITM also controls attacker-chosen text inside the emitted HTML. Flagged at LOW confidence for Shape C specifically: unlike lead 1 there is NO hedge comment or other marker that a protection was deliberately disabled or reverted here, so the parallel-path inconsistency is the only signal and this may simply be stale demo code rather than a reverted fix.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): The code the lead describes is present verbatim and I re-derived it myself. ParserResource.java:53 constructs `new URL(\"http://api.flickr.com/services/rest/?...&api_key=221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77&...\")` and opens it at :54 over plaintext HTTP, transmitting a hardcoded API key in the query string (CWE-319, with a CWE-798 element). It IS reachable from a real web entry point: the class is `@Path(\"/parser\")` (:39) with `@GET doGet()` (:42), registered in `DemoApplication.getClasses()` (:32) under `@ApplicationPath(\"/\")`; my graph query returned two EntryPoint nodes {kind:\"handler\", exposure:\"reachable\"} bound to `ParserResource.doGet` and its `StreamingOutput$0.write` lambda, with lines 53 and 74 both reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=3. The downstream reflection is also real: line 74 concatenates farm/server/id/secret -- each from `parser.getString()` at :69/:73/:78/:82 -- into an `<img src=\"...\">` attribute, and I read all 92 lines of the file and found no escaping/encoding call, so an MITM who controls the plaintext JSON response controls attacker-chosen text inside the emitted HTML. However, I REJECT the analyst's Shape-C framing (a deliberately reverted/disabled protection). The three \"parallel paths\" are three different vendor APIs of different vintages (ParserResource (c) 2012, TwitterObjectSearch (c) 2013), not three implementations of one operation; the https/http split is consistent with what each vendor's API supported in 2012, and there is no hedge comment or revert marker anywhere. Severity is capped at LOW by three facts I verified: (a) the `demos` module is NOT in the shipped artifact -- root pom.xml:438-442 lists only impl/rest/bundles, demos is in the `all` profile (pom.xml:466-469) and explicitly excluded by the `oss-release` profile (pom.xml:496-499, comment \"to make sure that release profile deactivates building samples\"); (b) the api_key is already published in this open-source repo, so cleartext transmission adds no marginal secret exposure; (c) exploitation requires a developer to deliberately build and deploy the sample WAR and an attacker on-path to api.flickr.com. Note: `exploit_search` returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit-reference grounding was available; this finding is not a dependency-version CVE so that does not change the verdict."
          },
